A||N||26||DaRkLoRD||ARGH! sorry.. gotta vent a bit.||||8||
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||12-13-2001||01:20 PM||||I was hired to do document processing, back in july.. They offered $20/hr, plus benefits, plus free training in electronics, which is what I was going to school for anyways.. so I got my 3rd semester tuition money back from my school, and took this job full time. as soon as I accepted the job, they cut the salary in half and eliminated ALL the benefits... but I needed the money desperately... when I proved to be able to do the job to their satisfaction, they started giving me more complicated tasks, which then led to IT support.. that's what I normally do. The problem is, they're still only paying me $10/hr CDN. (salary for IT is $35/hr)<P>I was promised bonuses for the non-document processing work I did.. haven't seen a single one yet. I was promised a raise to $15/hr.. they had a big meeting yesterday afternoon to discuss business stuff.. didn't get the raise.. so this morning they said "ok, we think you deserve $12/hr".. so I said fine, better than $10.. right? <P>this afternoon they made new contracts (we're all contractors, instead of employees.. something about taxes......) anyway, it said $10/hr. they claim they don't remember ever saying I would get $12/hr... then they asked if I felt they'd ever burned me on a business deal.. I said no, but the answer is most certainly yes.<P>Anyway, once again they've made promises about bonuses and raises... but I'm not gonna hold my breath.<P>I create formatted training documents based on the rough notes they give me.. I set up a video tour system that allows people to take a virtual tour of the reactor vault, and other inaccessible areas of the station (I work at a nuclear training center btw) I've created powerpoint presentations for all their courses.. I set up the computers here.. I also do a lot of high-end graphics editing.. I went up on a 10 foot ladder (I've received no safety training.) to run network cables.. no mask, no safety glasses.. fiberglass insulation everywhere. I got fiberglass in my eye.. still hurts, that was a week ago. I'm not really afraid of heights, but I don't like it much.. I went up on the ladder (not safe, it was wobbling a bit) and I only asked my boss one thing, please hold the ladder... as soon as I got up there, he said "if you need me I'll be out in the shop".<BR>On top of that, they like joking around a lot.. when they're not hitting on every woman in the building that is.. they also like to joke around amongst themselves about how if someone is making $10/hr they might as well just stay home, as it's next to nothing.. they say this right in front of me.<BR>They also give me a ride into work in the morning.. they tell me what time to be ready, (7:50 this morning..) and I was on time.. at 7:40, they were banging on my door telling me to hurry up... I'm beginning to think they never keep their word about anything. They constantly joke around with me, saying stuff like "that's why we pay you the big bucks!" whenever I do something or figure something out for them.. they say since I make so much money, I should buy them beer.. plenty of other examples, but this message is long enough already... they act like they know me well enough to joke around at my expense.. but they most certainly don't... <P>my parents get ticked off any time I even hint at any of this stuff.. easy for them to say I should be glad I have a job, they don't hafta work here.. I work 40 hours a week (get paid for 35 usually....) and my bosses take credit for the work I do, and then get big bonuses... all the while saying they can't afford to give me a raise.... (yet they have an "entertainment" budget, for when they go to the bar every night...<BR>I'm sick of this job.. but I need the money. I used to make $25/hr doing this type of work, but business slowed down and I had to take this job.. There aren't ANY other good jobs in my area. believe me, I've looked...<P>why the hell does everyone think they're doing me the biggest goddamn favor in the world, when they're actually screwing me over? It's been like this for years, with whatever I do. <BR>Is there something about me that makes people think they can treat me like sh*t or something???<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||8
Z||000001||loafer87gt||12-13-2001||01:32 PM||||I'd tell them where to go. They obviously don't respect your talent and skills, and I personally would not want to work with the people you described. There is a difference between working your way up in a business and being taken advantage of. For the sort of work you described I think $10 an hour is an insult, my friend flipping burgers at the university makes $14!! It would be different if you had a bunch of of bonuses such as health coverage etc, but as is, I think they are far underpaying you. Do you know who your main boss is? I would have a talk with him and tell him how you feel and see what his/her response is.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||12-13-2001||01:32 PM||||tape record them with a walkman type unit<BR>get the promises on record!!!!!!!!<BR>TELL THEM YOU GOT A BETTER OFFER do they want to beat it?????????<BR>every day look for a better job NEVER STOP LOOKING, NETWORK your contacks to look for you.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000003||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||01:33 PM||||VENT AWAY!!!<P>I know exactly what you are going thru..<P>I worked for a comunications company for about 12 years.. then all of a sudden, slowly, month by month, things changed.<P>Less bennies, hardass mngmt., downsizing, etc.<P>I left, took what I had in 401k, stock options, and started my own roofing company, (something I learned from my Grandad, and what I used to do..)<P>Being in business, you get to see that the government, State and Federal suck you dry on everything. <P>Also having employees make everything much more complex. I just basically do ALL the work myself and reap the benefits.<P>Contracting out just saves businesses money bexause they don't have to worry about insurance, comp, lawsuits, and most importantly save MONEY !<P>It's not what the USA used to be!!! You can't just be content working for someone else. If you have a knack, try going out on your own..it's not easy at first, but all the money is yours!<P>Don't be content being an "employee" be an "Owner"!!!!<P>Good Luck, and I know you can do better!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over, runs smooth, shifts crisp!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||BN Boomer||12-13-2001||01:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>Is there something about me that makes people think they can treat me like sh*t or something???<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You let yourself get pushed around too much. I would have told them to piss up a rope if they suddenly renegged on the benefit package and cut the pay to HALF of what had been agreed to when I accepted the job. $10 an hour Canadian? Geez, you can probably do better than that at McDonald's. In your willingness to accept the job with such drastic reductions, they probably sensed a lack of motivation, low self esteem, unwillingness to stand up for yourself, or desparation, any of which they are exploiting and using to take adnavtage of you. And now that you have a history of simply taking whatever they throw at you, you may find it unlikely to get any better. If you are truly that desperate for money and the job market is so tight that you must suffer this for $10 and hour, then I guess you got to do what you got to do. Personally, I think it's time to stand up for yourself, find a better job, or go back to school.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChopTop||12-13-2001||01:50 PM||||It's the proverbial, you said, they said.  Unless you get it in writing you're pretty much screwed.  What do you want?  What are your options?  If you demand a raise will they simply say, see ya. Or, will they say let's discuss it.  If they agree to a discussion list all the accomplishments you have performed along with any new skills you have mastered.  Basically what you saying is, I'm a valuable employee to your organization and it would be in your best financial best interest to pay me for my talents.  If nothing else I'd let management see you looking in the want ads and use a big red marker for the positions you're interested in.  Let people think your going on job interviews during your lunch hour.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BN Boomer||12-13-2001||01:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>It's not what the USA used to be!!! You can't just be content working for someone else. If you have a knack, try going out on your own..it's not easy at first, but all the money is yours!<P>Don't be content being an "employee" be an "Owner"!!!!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sure, you got a few billion laying around that I can borrow to buy my own railroad?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/en/locomotive/na_freight/sd70mac/images/sd70mac.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||12-13-2001||01:56 PM||||Better 10 an hour after being cheated than 0 an hour and being fu**ed.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||02:11 PM||||You like trains?<P>I have modeled trains and layouts since 1976.<P>My specialty, B&M steam era! Must have most every engine and car ever produced!<P>Burlington Northern Santa Fe? I like just plain old Santa FE.. But see, even the railroads have to deal with the new, tight, streamlined America!!!<P>Don't work for someone else!!! Be your OWN person, live the dream! It can be done!!<P>(O.K. maybe you have to work for someone else while getting established, but DO SOMETHING!!<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||BN Boomer||12-13-2001||02:16 PM||||I work for the BNSF. In the operating department as a switchman, brakeman,and conductor. Currently foreman on a hump job. My user "Boomer" is railroad slang. I'm former Burlington Northern and don't much care for the Santa Fe. But I'm still pretty content working for some one else.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||02:21 PM||||You know, I guess there are some jobs that are good to have!!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'd like to do what you do.. but It's probably not what it used to be.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Nice to hear from a railroad guy. <P>My rant was for the guy who was sick of his job BS!<p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||12-13-2001||02:28 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>...TELL THEM YOU GOT A BETTER OFFER do they want to beat it?????????</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, THERE'S a good way to get fired...nice advice there, Ray. <P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>every day look for a better job NEVER STOP LOOKING...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...At least you're not completely stupid...<P>Dark, sounds like you've been bitten by the same bug that got me. I got on at State Farm (actually under contract as well...can I say State Farm here?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) anyway, I was making $37K...not bad. Got an offer from another co for $55K for the same position (heck, same desk and everything!), but the old co was telling us we had "non-compete" clauses...we didn't. Anyway, they found out (through another contractor) that I had an offer, and LAID ME OFF 1 DAY BEFORE MY CONTRACT WAS UP! This killed the other deal, and I've been laid off since July. This was in IT Support as well...IT has been so DEAD this year! Unemployment runs out in the next 4 weeks, and I'll have to find something just to try to get by...Probably end up with 2 jobs or more...got 2 kids and one on the way (oops!). <P>Anyway, I feel for you. Keep plugging away, and keep looking...the market is bound to come back, just don't use them for a reference...places like this tend to screw you on your way out as well...I know. <P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0NgAAAMsSN*Wryq60N*CeWB!hXjfeIaMEc1YxPH9aPzaIXm!17ZpdBFE0Yk*d*dHNFG*GR3SFyLa8KJgh1IKe2Q/xmas-sig.jpg">        <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="red">Merry Christmas!</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B><BR>Photoshop rendition of planned modifications<BR>AIM ~ Michael Blue 72</I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||12-13-2001||02:29 PM||||If they want contract labor, YOU tell them what to pay you or your gone, unless they can make a sweeter offer. Id def be starting to look elsewhere. If your making them money, they cant afford to let you go.JMO||||reg||1
Z||000013||avengador1||12-13-2001||08:24 PM||||Look for a job somewhere's else. Always get promises in writting, that way if they reneg you can sue them. You might even find a better paying job doing something else. Twelve years ago when I was between jobs (fired) I took a job installing car electronics. It didn't pay much but I had a mortgage, car payments, and a wife to take care of. Eventually I got another job, doing something entirely different again. The best time to look for a new job is while you are still employed, just tell your potential new employer that you don't want them to contact your employer cause it will cause you trouble.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||SQL||12-13-2001||08:54 PM||||I had to shovel a lot of ****  before I found a job that paid well and I liked doing.<BR>I worked as a welder for 15 years (4 of that was in the Navy) I liked my job at the end, but welding is dangerous. I started going to school at night and now work at a local clinic. I am again working my way out of a pit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> the job is cool I get to play with a lot of neat stuff. I have never stopped looking at for a chance to better myself. I would suggest you go talk to your supervisor, tell them how you feel and show them your work. If they feel you are being paid for what you are doing start looking, don’t let them know about it because it sounds like you need to move on. It’s hard to keep positive when you are not treated fairly. When I was welding and would get pasted up for a promotion so they could hire a new employee at a higher pay then me, I would just smile and come home get cleaned up and go to school, I spent a lot of time studying in the can.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> Don't worry it sounds like you are motivated, stay that way you will get what you want.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||12-13-2001||09:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>  ...At least you're not completely stupid...<P>Dark, sounds like you've been bitten by the same bug that got me. I got on at State Farm (actually under contract as well...can I say State Farm here?!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) anyway, I was making $37K...not bad. Got an offer from another co for $55K for the same position (heck, same desk and everything!), but the old co was telling us we had "non-compete" clauses...we didn't. Anyway, they found out (through another contractor) that I had an offer, and LAID ME OFF 1 DAY BEFORE MY CONTRACT WAS UP! This killed the other deal, and I've been laid off since July. This was in IT Support as well...IT has been so DEAD this year! Unemployment runs out in the next 4 weeks, and I'll have to find something just to try to get by...Probably end up with 2 jobs or more...got 2 kids and one on the way (oops!). <P>Anyway, I feel for you. Keep plugging away, and keep looking...the market is bound to come back, just don't use them for a reference...places like this tend to screw you on your way out as well...I know. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Triad: this is IT... That's how it's DONE. Ray is 100% right, here... If you have qualifications, you use them. To replace him with a certified tech would cost PLENTY more than to give him a raise. <BR>If you've worked with them for more than 6 months, your EI should cover your school, if they decide to look elsewhere. <BR>Sounds like a **** -job  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000016||Jaygee79||12-13-2001||11:17 PM||||I agree with Ray.  You need to make sure you get hard copy proof of any promises they make to you.  And continue looking for something else.||||reg||1
Z||000017||BigBoyToys||12-13-2001||11:36 PM||||GO BACK TO SCHOOL!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||12
Z||000018||DaRkLoRD||12-13-2001||11:53 PM||||I'm working at this job to make enough money to afford to go back to school..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I miss college like crazy.. not so much the work, but the social aspect.. mmm.. co-ed dorms. hehe..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||TRiAD||12-14-2001||01:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B> Triad: this is IT... That's how it's DONE. Ray is 100% right, here... If you have qualifications, you use them. To replace him with a certified tech would cost PLENTY more than to give him a raise. <BR>If you've worked with them for more than 6 months, your EI should cover your school, if they decide to look elsewhere. <BR>Sounds like a **** -job   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You OBVIOUSLY haven't been in this boat, like I have. <BR>Let me say this plainly; <B>if they know you're looking for another job, or are considering other (even imaginary) offers, they'll just CAN you before you can find anything else, then you'll have NO INCOME till you find something else.</B><P>Yes, OBVIOUSLY, get promises in writing, (BTW, TAPING CONVERSATIONS W/O PERMISSIONS IS ILLEGAL! ...and inadmissable in court), but other than that, the rest was all off. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||12-14-2001||01:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> You OBVIOUSLY haven't been in this boat, like I have. <BR>Let me say this plainly; [b]if they know you're looking for another job, or are considering other (even imaginary) offers, they'll just CAN you before you can find anything else, then you'll have NO INCOME till you find something else.</B><P>Yes, OBVIOUSLY, get promises in writing, (BTW, TAPING CONVERSATIONS W/O PERMISSIONS IS ILLEGAL! ...and inadmissable in court), but other than that, the rest was all off. [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Been in "the boat" since before high-school. Having no official certification is a GOOD thing, for a couple of reasons. First of all, any self-respecting CERTIFIED professional will be charging upwards of 50/hour. Me, I'd be going from 10/hour to 20 or so. After that, there's no use, and you REALLY have to CYA. However, at the entry-level stage, what the are looking for is CHEAP SKILLED LABOUR. If they don't have to shell out for the big guys, they won't. Even if it means *gasp* giving you a raise. Again, once you reach a certain point, your advantage is GONE.<P>There is a reason why I jumped off the boat and swam for shore. I HATE the idea of a diploma with at "Best-Before" date on it. <P>In Canada, this is called wrongful dismissal. They can word it however they like, but our EI still covers it. In this lad's case, they will pay for him to go back to school. While you collect from EI, you are also free to make proceedings against the company... Again, it's a big risk to the employer. I firmly believe that at that level, he a) doesn't have much to lose, and b) DOES have some leverage.  But caution is in order  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000021||BN Boomer||12-14-2001||01:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>I'm working at this job to make enough money to afford to go back to school..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I miss college like crazy.. not so much the work, but the social aspect.. mmm.. co-ed dorms. hehe..<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It would still be best to use this experience as a learning tool rather than waiting for the real world to slap you in the face again later on.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||fierospeeder||12-14-2001||02:13 AM||||i would quit right in the middle of a big project.<P>Theres a big ass difference between 10 and 20 dollars an hour.   <P>I get time and a half on sundays at my work.  And that makes a diffence on my paycheck.<P><BR>If management was making a buck, i would quit right away.  The pricks with the suits that drink coffee and read the newspaper in the john.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Little Duce Coupe||12-14-2001||10:53 AM||||Just some thoughts, only personal opinion.<P>~ The boss usually takes the credit thats why he is the boss. A good one will always reward you though.<P>~ Never quit a job unless you got another one on the line.  It looks bad even if they do treat you like S@#$.<P>~ Never stop looking for a better one even if you do like where you are.<P>~ If you can go to another job, it is much more fun/satifying to leave them in the lurch of a big project.<P>~ If you do find another job you are willing to take, then let them step up to the table to see if they want you to stay.  If they do don't be SHY ask for everything they promissed amd MORE, this way it is bargained to where you WANT to be.<P>~ This is unfortualtely life, it happens more often than not and the other posters are right.... the more you let them do you the more there gonna do you.<P>~The school things is a good idea too, let them help you pay the bill for a while then when your done they can kiss your @#$ goodbye.<P>Best of Luck,<BR>"Never burn bridges, but when the time comes put it too the torch and enjoy the warmth"<P>Just a thought||||reg||1
Z||000024||TRiAD||12-14-2001||11:01 AM||||Thanks Duce, this is what I'm trying to say.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||wkayl||12-14-2001||08:22 PM||||Just an idea.  If you are a contract worker, would that make the videos and power point presentations you made yours?  ||||reg||1
Z||000026||DaRkLoRD||12-14-2001||09:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by wkayl:<BR><B>Just an idea.  If you are a contract worker, would that make the videos and power point presentations you made yours?  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wish.. we're developing a new training program for nuclear operators.. there are tens of thousands of old training documents, system flowsheets, diagrams, etc. and we're going thru them, and selecting the best stuff, and reformatting it in electronic format.. (that's where the scanning and image editing comes in)<P>The documents that are created from scratch belong to my bosses, retired training managers who formed a consulting business and made a contract with their former employer.. the stuff that I've just scanned and cleaned up, but not changed, belongs to Bruce Power/Ontario Power Generation (formerly Ontario Hydro)<P>so.. to answer your question.. no, I don't own anything.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
